#{[17592186226200 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice transforming exponential functions, rewriting the transformed functions using the laws of exponents."] [17592186226201 1029 "Another common theme in the math section of the SAT is constructing exponential functions from a given description. Here, we'll practice building exponential models from descriptions that include initial values, growth factors, or percentage decreases."] [17592186226202 1029 "Questions that ask you to determine inputs for a given output in quadratic functions are a common feature of the math section of the SAT. In this lesson, we'll gain valuable practice with these problems."] [17592186226203 1029 "Quadratic models describing real-world phenomena often have specific features that can be interpreted.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice translating quadratic models into plain-English statements."] [17592186226204 1029 "Geometry problems that involve dimensions, area, or perimeter often lead to quadratic equations.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll practice translating geometric descriptions into quadratic equations and solving them."] [17592186226205 1029 "Questions about extreme values of quadratic functions in standard form often test whether we know how to find a vertex.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice the efficient application of the vertex formula."] [17592186226206 1029 "Problems involving extreme values of quadratic functions often test whether we can quickly identify the vertex of a parabola from its factored form.\n\nHere, we’ll practice efficiently locating maximums and minimums of quadratic functions presented in factored form."] [17592186226207 1029 "Problems that relate two or more numbers through multiplication or addition often lead to quadratic equations. These problems test our ability to translate a word problem into a quadratic equation and solve it.\n\nIn this lesson, we'll practice forming and solving quadratic equations that model number relationships. The resulting quadratic equations typically have quite large coefficients, which often make the quadratic formula an efficient solution method."] [17592186226208 1029 "When presented with real-world data on vertical motion, we can often fit it to a quadratic model using vertex form. In this topic, we'll practice doing just that."] [17592186226209 1029 "Questions about multiplying binomials test the fluency with the distributive property and the ability to organize terms correctly to avoid sign or arithmetic errors.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice expanding products of binomials of different forms, simplifying the results, and matching coefficients or terms to solve for unknown values."] [17592186226210 1029 "Questions involving distributive laws often appear on the math section of the SAT exam. These questions check whether we can correctly expand, combine, and simplify expressions.\n\nHere, we’ll practice applying the distributive property to polynomial expressions, simplifying them, and identifying or comparing coefficients to find missing values."] [17592186226211 1029 "The difference of squares formula often appears in questions that require quick recognition of algebraic patterns.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice identifying and factoring differences of squares, including cases with coefficients, fractions, and multiple variables."] [17592186226212 1029 "Factoring by a greatest common factor (GCF) is often used as a step in solving problems that contain polynomial expressions.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice spotting numeric and variable GCFs in polynomials and using them to factor the expressions."] [17592186226213 1029 "Questions involving factoring trinomials are common on the math section of the SAT exam. Recognizing the corresponding patterns can help us solve equations and simplify expressions.\n\nHere, we’ll practice factoring different types of trinomials, including perfect squares and more general quadratic expressions."] [17592186226214 1029 "Factoring higher-order polynomials tests our ability to recognize patterns beyond simple quadratics.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice identifying and factoring polynomial patterns, including higher powers and nested differences of squares."] [17592186226215 1029 "Questions about composite solids often ask to combine or compare volumes of familiar shapes, such as hemispheres, cones, cylinders, and prisms.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice decomposing the figure into parts, apply the correct volume formulas to each piece, and then add or subtract to get the final result."] [17592186226216 1029 "Problems involving borders or frames often test the ability to express areas with unknown widths using algebraic equations.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice modeling these geometric relationships and solving for missing dimensions."] [17592186226217 1029 "The math problems often involve modeling area or perimeter using algebraic functions. These questions combine geometric understanding with algebraic manipulation.\n\nMastering this connection between shapes and equations takes practice, but it’s a powerful way to boost both reasoning speed and accuracy when dealing with geometry problems."] [17592186226218 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, we're often asked to create a function for surface area or volume directly from a description.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice constructing and interpreting functions that represent surface area and volume, turning real-world descriptions into precise mathematical formulas."] [17592186226219 1029 "Problems involving inscribed squares and circles often test our understanding of how geometric relationships between these shapes connect.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice translating between circle and square parameters (radius, side, area, and circumference) to solve multi-step geometry problems."] [17592186226220 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, questions about circles inscribed in equilateral triangles often depend on 30-60-90 right-triangle reasoning. In this lesson, we’ll practice setting up these relationships and solving problems."] [17592186226221 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, we’ll often see problems that hide special triangles inside rectangles, squares, or circles. These questions test our ability to spot these special triangles and use their ratios to find unknown lengths or areas.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice recognizing these patterns and setting up the correct equations."] [17592186226222 1029 "Inscribed‐circle problems often ask us to switch quickly between a circle’s radius or diameter and a square’s side.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice setting up these relationships and solving multi-step geometric problems."] [17592186226223 1029 "Problems involving equilateral triangles inscribed in a circle often boil down to recalling 30-60-90 triangle facts.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice setting up these relationships and solving related problems."] [17592186226224 1029 "The math section of the SAT exam sometimes asks to evaluate simple trigonometric functions at large angles. These evaluations are made by reducing the argument of the trigonometric function using periodicity.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice reducing large angles to their coterminal or reference angles, identifying the relevant quadrants, and determining the correct sign and value of the trigonometric ratio."] [17592186226225 1029 "Questions that require us to solve quadratic equations with unknown parameters show up regularly on the math section of the SAT exam.\n\nIn this topic, we'll build speed by practicing systematic moves for solving these kinds of problems."] [17592186226226 1029 "Solving quadratic equations by factoring tests the ability to recognize when a quadratic can be factored quickly into linear factors.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice rewriting, factoring, and solving quadratic equations efficiently, developing the fluency needed to identify patterns and avoid time-consuming methods like the quadratic formula."] [17592186226227 1029 "This topic focuses on understanding how the structure of a quadratic expression reveals useful information about its behavior.\n\nBy recognizing connections across different forms, we’ll be able to extract relationships between coefficients, factors, and intercepts."] [17592186226228 1029 "Quadratic equations have exactly one real solution when their discriminant equals zero.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice identifying and using this condition."] [17592186226229 1029 "Quadratic equations have real solutions when their discriminant is nonnegative, and *distinct* real solutions when their discriminant is positive.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice identifying and using these conditions."] [17592186226230 1029 "Quadratic equations have no real solutions when their discriminant is negative.\n\nIn this topic, we'll practice identifying and using this condition."] [17592186226231 1029 "This topic focuses on solving nonlinear equations that involve multiple variables. We'll practice isolating variables in radical and quadratic equations, rearranging formulas, and applying the laws of exponents when working with radicals."] [17592186226232 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice solving systems of nonlinear equations involving square roots, exponentials, and absolute values."] [17592186226233 1029 "An important concept that features in the math section of the SAT exam is identifying when a line and a parabola intersect at exactly one point.\n\nIn this topic, we will determine unknown parameters in quadratic systems with one solution involving horizontal or sloped lines, as well as cases where both linear and quadratic equations within a system contain parameters."] [17592186226234 1029 "Common questions on the math section of the SAT exam involve working with exponential functions written in equivalent forms.\n\nYou'll need to be able to analyze how different representations display key quantities, such as constant terms, coefficients, or bases, that correspond to the function's maximum, minimum, or given output values."] [17592186226235 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice converting exponential models to different time units. We will adjust equations to reflect new time measures, compute percentage increases or decreases over specified intervals, and determine time periods associated with particular percentage changes."] [17592186226236 1029 "A common theme on the SAT math section is understanding how exponential functions behave, and this can include cases with restricted domains. In particular, you will need to identify when they have maximum or minimum values, determine their ranges, and describe whether they are increasing or decreasing over those domains."] [17592186226237 1029 "A common theme on the math section of the SAT is working with exponential functions that contain one or multiple unknown parameters.\nIn this topic, we'll practice solving for these unknowns using information such as points on the curve or the y-intercept."] [17592186226238 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice performing horizontal and vertical translations on polynomials."] [17592186226239 1029 "Problems involving the symmetry of quadratic functions often test how well we can reason about a parabola without graphing it.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice using these patterns to draw logical conclusions about a quadratic function’s structure and behavior."] [17592186226240 1029 "In this topic, we'll use our knowledge of the factor theorem to draw conclusions about unknown coefficients in quadratic equations."] [17592186226241 1029 "Questions about quadratic functions in vertex form often focus on identifying or interpreting the maximum or minimum value of a parabola quickly.\n\nBelow, we’ll practice reading vertex form directly and using it to determine both the location and the value of a parabola’s highest or lowest point."] [17592186226242 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, questions that describe physical relationships usually test our ability to translate written definitions into mathematical formulas.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice turning descriptions of physical phenomena into equations, using the given definitions to solve for unknown variables, and reinforcing formula reasoning through applied examples."] [17592186226243 1029 "In this topic, we will determine unknown parameters in quadratic systems with zero or two solutions involving horizontal or sloped lines, as well as cases where both equations of the system contain parameters."] [17592186226244 1029 "Solving quadratic equations with leading coefficients by factoring tests the ability to recognize when a quadratic can be factored quickly into linear factors.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice factoring and solving quadratic equations with leading coefficients efficiently, developing the fluency needed to identify patterns and avoid time-consuming methods like the quadratic formula."] [17592186226245 1029 "In this topic, we'll focus on recognizing and using structure to solve nonlinear systems of equations. We will practice substituting expressions with one or two variables and applying patterns such as the difference of squares to find solutions efficiently."] [17592186226246 1029 "In this topic, we'll use our knowledge of polynomial graphs to determine their properties, such as the number of roots and valid outputs of polynomials over particular intervals. We'll also practice identifying the factored form of a polynomial from its graph."] [17592186226247 1029 "In this topic, we'll use our knowledge of polynomial transformations to identify properties of transformed polynomials, including their roots, vertices, and tables of values."] [17592186230066 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, we may encounter questions that ask to add or subtract rational expressions, often with unlike denominators.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice turning sums and differences into a single simplified fraction by creating least common denominators, as well as distributing and canceling common factors."] [17592186230067 1029 "Questions about expressing ratios as percentages test the ability to interpret part–whole relationships and convert fractions or ratios into percent form. In this topic, we’ll practice performing these conversions."] [17592186230068 1029 "Questions about finding totals from percentages test whether we can reverse the usual percent-of-a-number process. Sometimes these problems appear in real-world contexts.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice setting up and solving equations to find the total from a known part and a percentage."] [17592186230069 1029 "Questions about applying a percentage change test whether we can correctly use percentage increases or decreases in real-world and algebraic settings.\n\nIn this lesson, we’ll practice applying percentage change to calculate new values, model practical scenarios, and strengthen our fluency with common exam-style percent word problems."] [17592186230070 1029 "Problems about finding original values from the percentage change test our ability to work backward from a percentage increase or decrease. These problems often appear in real-world contexts.\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice reversing percent increases and decreases to find original quantities."] [17592186230071 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice determining percentage change in both abstract and real-world contexts."] [17592186230072 1029 "Problems about successive percentage changes often involve chained growth or loss (such as multi-step discounts, compound price adjustments, or percentage comparisons).\n\nIn this topic, we’ll practice working with multiple percentage changes."] [17592186230073 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice calculating the median of datasets with odd and even numbers of values, and determining possible medians based on the data's distribution."] [17592186230074 1029 "A key concept that often features on the math section of the SAT exam is understanding the relationship between the mean of a dataset and the sum of its values.\n\nIn this topic, we will practice expressing the mean as a fraction of the sum, combining means from different groups, and using sums to find unknown or constrained data values."] [17592186230075 1029 "An important concept on the math section of the SAT exam is understanding how the mean and median relate to each other.\n\nHere, we will determine which measures can be calculated, find means and medians of consecutive integers, and use relationships between them to find missing values."] [17592186230076 1029 "This topic provides practice in constructing and analyzing frequency tables. We will practice summarizing datasets, interpreting frequency values, determining ranges and extreme values, and working with joint frequency tables to compare categories."] [17592186230077 1029 "On the math section of the SAT exam, an important concept is finding and comparing measures of center from frequency tables.\n\nIn this topic, we will practice calculating means and medians, comparing them when frequencies are unknown, and ordering measures of centrality using the given data."] [17592186230078 1029 "This topic will strengthen our ability to calculate probabilities and their complements from datasets."] [17592186230079 1029 "Questions that require adding or subtracting rational expressions with variables and common factors in their denominators test fluency in recognizing common factors. In this topic, we’ll practice combining rational expressions involving common monomial and binomial denominators, including cases where we must first factor some of the denominators."] [17592186230080 1029 "In this topic, we'll practice calculating and interpreting conditional probabilities using data from two-way frequency tables. We'll also find missing frequencies given a conditional probability."] [17592186230081 1029 "In this topic, we will strengthen our skills in calculating conditional probabilities using data presented in two-way percentage tables or described in word problems."]}
